    {
      "id": "math-i-binomial-theorem-19",
      "prompt": "In the induction proof of the binomial theorem, why is Pascal's identity needed?",
      "answer": "After multiplying \\((a+b)^N\\) by \\((a+b)\\), terms with the same power \\(a^{N-k+1}b^k\\) come from two neighbouring coefficients:\n\\[\n\\binom{N}{k}+\\binom{N}{k-1}\n\\]\nPascal's identity turns this into\n\\[\n\\binom{N+1}{k}\n\\]\nwhich is the required coefficient for power \\(N+1\\).",
      "rating": "****",
      "paperTags": ["term-1"]
