    {
      "id": "math-i-binomial-theorem-20",
      "prompt": "Prove the binomial theorem step from \\(N\\) to \\(N+1\\) in outline.",
      "answer": "Assume\n\\[\n(a+b)^N=\\sum_{k=0}^{N}\\binom{N}{k}a^{N-k}b^k\n\\]\nThen multiply by \\(a+b\\). The \\(a\\)-part and \\(b\\)-part give neighbouring coefficients for each middle term. Pascal's identity combines them:\n\\[\n\\binom{N}{k}+\\binom{N}{k-1}=\\binom{N+1}{k}\n\\]\nThe end terms are \\(a^{N+1}\\) and \\(b^{N+1}\\), so the result is the binomial theorem for \\(N+1\\).",
